In practice one rarely runs the proof; one computes ranks. For an eigenvalue $\lambda$ put $d_j = \dim\ker(A - \lambda I)^j = n - \operatorname{rank}(A - \lambda I)^j$. Then:

- the number of blocks for $\lambda$ is $d_1$, the geometric multiplicity;
- the total size of the blocks for $\lambda$ is its algebraic multiplicity;
- the number of blocks of size exactly $j$ is $2d_j - d_{j-1} - d_{j+1}$;
- the largest block size for $\lambda$ is the smallest $j$ with $d_j = d_{j+1}$.

Some consequences. $A$ is diagonalisable exactly when all blocks have size $1$. Each block $J_k(\lambda)$ satisfies $(J_k(\lambda) - \lambda I)^k = 0$ and $k$ is at most the algebraic multiplicity of $\lambda$, so $p_A(A) = 0$: this is the Cayley–Hamilton theorem. The smallest-degree monic polynomial killing $A$ (the minimal polynomial) is $\prod_\lambda(t - \lambda)^{k_\lambda}$, with $k_\lambda$ the largest block size for $\lambda$; so $A$ is diagonalisable if and only if some polynomial with distinct roots kills it.

For powers and exponentials, the block formula above gives
$$e^{tJ_k(\lambda)} = e^{\lambda t}\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\frac{t^j}{j!}N^j.$$
This is the source of the solutions $te^{\lambda t}, t^2e^{\lambda t}, \dots$ of [[ode-linear|linear differential equations]] with repeated characteristic roots, and of terms $m\lambda^m$ in linear recurrences. It also shows that $A^m \to 0$ as $m \to \infty$ exactly when every eigenvalue has $|\lambda| < 1$, because $\binom{m}{j}\lambda^{m-j} \to 0$ for each fixed $j$ when $|\lambda| < 1$; conversely, if $Av = \lambda v$ with $v \ne 0$ and $|\lambda| \ge 1$, then $A^mv = \lambda^mv$ does not tend to $0$.

